hi there Steve Kaufman here and today  I'm going to talk about a subject that  has come up before and that is fluency  in three months I'm going to talk about  this for a variety of reasons but the  basically the trigger why I'm talking  about it now is because my friend Benny  the Irish polyglot has announced his  most recent project which is to achieve  a b2 level in Japanese in three months  he's going to do this in Valencia Spain  where he's studying Japanese in  anticipation of going there after three  months I don't remember how long he's  going to spend in Japan when he goes  there  so I watched the video I'm gonna leave a  link to the video then he is very  anxious to have you watched the video  and subscribe to his channel so I'm  helping him achieve a little bit more  you know notoriety fame whatever which  I'm happy to do because Benny is a great  motivator and if you watch this maybe it  video you will come away I think as I  did with the feeling that here's a man  who is encouraging people to learn  languages who doesn't he's not afraid to  show his own mistakes or shortcomings he  has a lot of enthusiasm and he's showing  people a way to learn a language so that  the message fundamentally is positive  however when it comes to the actual  substance of the message in other words  that you can achieve B 2 which is  fluency in 3 months in a language like  Japanese which is very different from  any language that Benny knows um do I  think that is possible  No do I think is realistic no and I will  explain why and I even think that while  it Benny carries it off because he's so  enthusiastic in this video but overall  as a message to language learners I  don't think it's a very constructive one  now I want to first of all tell you what  b2 in the car  in European framework of reference or  frame of reference whatever it's called  b2 corresponds to it is can understand  the main ideas of complex texts on both  concrete and abstract topics including  technical discussions in his or her  field of specialization can interact  with the degree of fluency and  spontaneity that makes regular  interaction with native speakers quite  possible without strain for either party  without strain for either party can  produce clear detailed text on a wide  range of subjects and explain a  viewpoint on a topical issue giving the  advantages and disadvantages of various  options is this achievable in 3 months  sitting in Valencia studying Japanese no  in fact Benny more-or-less admits this  in his video and he sort of says failure  is an option in other words he is  encouraging himself to learn he set this  unachievable goal and he's shooting for  the stars and he's going to work very  hard that part of it is very good I have  no doubt that he will work very hard and  I have no doubt that he will learn a lot  of Japanese and that he will arrive in  Japan with you know a great advantage  and probably further along than many  foreigners who have lived in Japan for  many many years for all these things I  salute him a great thing to be doing but  the message that you can achieve this  level of fluency what I just described  in three months it to me to me is almost  a distortion of language learning  what's the hurry what's the hurry in  fact to achieve that level of fluency  you have to have a lot of experience  with the language with the people with  the culture you have to understand  context there's a lot of things that you  simply cannot achieve no matter how hard  you work you know in a period of 3  months I think the language learning  process itself is enjoyable and I think  one of the things that we need to do is  to find ways of learning languages that  are enjoyable as Alex  our Gaia's said in a video which I'm  going to talk about in another video of  mine where he talks about polyglot Ori  he says that that is a number of very  interesting things that language  learning is fundamentally an  autodidactic process of interaction with  the language you yourself have to  interact with the language teachers can  encourage can provide feedback but  ultimately the successful language  learner is reacting in a self-motivated  way with the language that's how he  learns he also our genious also points  out that many polyglots such as alex  such as myself such as Benny all have  techniques on how to do this  autodidactic process and while there  will be many points in common there will  also be points or techniques or methods  that are different and this is going to  depend on what we like to do  circumstances time available and so  forth and we'll see this here with Benny  Benny's focuses on speaking he's always  said to speak from day one and here he  says he going to be speaking from day  one however many hours a day one or two  with his Japanese tutor and he will also  be studying he shows he got some books  in the background and he'll be working  with flashcards but the motivation for  him is to do all of this stuff so that  he can then speak a little more the next  day which is fine I would take a  different approach I would focus more on  listening and reading initially building  up my vocabulary and my ability to  understand to a point where actually I  can have a more meaningful discussion  with a tutor but you know the key thing  in language learning is a self-directed  autodidactic process of interaction with  the language you're obviously going to  want to do the things that you like to  do and the things that work for you I am  sure that I mean I see some of the  methods for example that Luka uses he  translates from the language he's  learning  into his own language and then back into  the target language again I'm sure  that's an excellent at a very effective  way of learning we also have Alex who is  our gayest who is very fond of shadowing  which means reading text out loud while  walking and which I'm sure and many  people claim this is very effective I  won't do either because I don't want to  I'm not writing a test I learn languages  for enjoyment I enjoy the process I have  for the last week or two been refreshing  my Italian and I'm having a ball I'm  listening to podcasts about you know  Charlamagne  Carla mano van GOG very interesting  podcast in Italian almost the word so I  don't need the text now I'm going to  start focusing again on Czech so I'll go  back to very interesting text that I  have with audio and I just like doing  that so and as long as I'm interacting  with the language I'm learning if I were  in Benny's shoes he's saying for example  he's not gonna read he's not interested  in reading he doesn't like reading my  approach would be to focus on building  up my comprehension therefore I would  want to read because reading is a very  good way of acquiring vocabulary and  learning about the country and the  culture in the history and so hot zone I  will build up I would have as my goal if  I were going to Japan in three months I  would build up my comprehension skills  for most because once I get to Japan  I'll have I think a lot more opportunity  to speak so I might have as was the case  with Romanian I might have you know  three hours a week or so of Skype  discussion not for the first month but  starting the second and third month but  mostly I want to build up my ability to  understand so when I get to Japan is in  the case of Benny I will stumble for the  first week or so but then I'll have lots  of chance to speak and if I understand  what people are saying and I've yet if I  can read things around me and if I can  read the newspaper and so  I'm going to be doing a lot better so  but that's you know I think the  important thing in language learning  what I kind of want to focus on here is  I think language learning is an  enjoyable activity for its own sake and  we should learn to do it in ways that we  find enjoyable so if it's enjoyable  what's the hurry  second of all I will pick up on  something that Alex our genious said in  his video and that is that he feels that  the sort of emphasis on quantifying in  language learning is not helpful in  other words how many languages do you  speak he says it's not a meaningful  question because he speaks them all at  different degrees of fluency and some of  them may lie dormant for a while he  can't just turn them on so he doesn't  like answering that question and he also  thinks that the emphasis on proficiency  tests testing our levels b2 b1 whatever  he thinks that's not so useful either  what he feels is useful is finding  efficient ways of learning and I would  add enjoyable ways of learning and  learning in ways that enable us to  maintain our languages I think this is  also another important point if you  learn a language for three months and  leave it it's very easy to lose it and  so you I feel it's important to have  ways of learning that you find enjoyable  so that you can always go back to them  and refresh and maintain and even  improve your languages and I know that  for example in Czech now which I've been  away from more or less for eight months  I go back and I grab a beginner book and  very quickly it all comes back to me and  I can almost hear again the recordings  that are used to listen to in my earlier  content and I'll do that for a couple of  days interspersed with the more  difficult stuff that I'm I'm doing it  and the whole process is enjoyable but  my check hasn't gone away it's kind of  subsided a bit but it can be very  quickly brought back because I learned  it through a process of a massive input  and exposure in a very enjoyable way so  it's not a chore it's not unpleasant I'm  not  in a hurry I'm enjoying it so following  up on that then rather than worrying  about how much we can cram into three  months how quickly we can learn how many  languages we speak all of this  quantifying I agree with Alex we should  focus on quality how efficiently how  enjoyably can we learn to what extent  can we learn languages in a way that we  don't lose them that we retain them and  can maintain them and I think those are  the important things and that kind of  activity is a life long endeavor we  never totally master a language we  shouldn't forget them either and we  gradually accumulate more and more  languages and I think they're I think  Benny has with his Japanese but you'll  follow up with a visit to Japan he'll  have there a base such as he has with  Chinese and that he can build on over  time in order to take those languages to  the level that he is at in his Romance  languages or in German it's going to  take a long time it's not going to be  three months and so to that extent I  think the the the idea that he  encourages people with his video and to  judge by the comments on his YouTube  channel people are encouraged and they  appreciate his energy and his honesty in  you know exposing his level in the  language so it is a positive message  message from that sense but for the many  many people who find that they you know  three months goes by very very quickly  if you start a new language especially a  very different language like Japanese  three months goes by very very quickly  and to even suggest that you could be at  a b2 level after three months is it in  real terms maybe not in terms of Benny's  objectives which is to stimulate  interest in language learning but from a  realistic point of view it's not  achievable so I don't want to start a  controversy but I just think it is  important to make the point for me to  state my beliefs and my beliefs  therefore  our that language learning is immensely  rewarding not only because of what we  were able to achieve ie potentially  fluency in the language but the actual  process itself it's a process of  discovery it's an enjoyable one the  brain learns but it learns slowly I look  forward to your comments thank you for  now bye
 

